1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,808
A swing in a park.

2
00:00:01,808 --> 00:00:03,006
A clock pendulum.

3
00:00:03,006 --> 00:00:05,478
A bob on a string in the physics lab.

4
00:00:05,878 --> 00:00:08,840
They all swing back and
forth in a fixed time.

5
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:10,382
What decides that time?

6
00:00:10,832 --> 00:00:15,398
Aarav ties his keys to a string in the
college corridor and lets it swing.

7
00:00:15,748 --> 00:00:17,505
No formula, no textbook.

8
00:00:17,505 --> 00:00:20,490
Can he still guess the
formula for one swing?

9
00:00:20,990 --> 00:00:21,890
Yes.

10
00:00:21,605 --> 00:00:23,452
Using only dimensions.

11
00:00:23,452 --> 00:00:25,069
Today we see how.

12
00:00:25,669 --> 00:00:30,752
Last time we learned: every term in a
correct equation has the same dimensions.

13
00:00:30,752 --> 00:00:32,790
That rule has three big uses.

14
00:00:33,190 --> 00:00:34,090
One.

15
00:00:33,664 --> 00:00:36,004
Check whether a formula can be right.

16
00:00:36,404 --> 00:00:37,304
Two.

17
00:00:36,911 --> 00:00:40,491
Convert a quantity from one
system of units to another.

18
00:00:40,891 --> 00:00:41,791
Three.

19
00:00:41,455 --> 00:00:44,636
Guess the form of a formula,
before any experiment.

20
00:00:44,636 --> 00:00:46,494
Let us start with the swing.

21
00:00:47,144 --> 00:00:49,085
Here is the corridor pendulum.

22
00:00:49,085 --> 00:00:52,408
The time for one full swing
is the time period, T.

23
00:00:52,758 --> 00:00:53,931
Watch the graph.

24
00:00:53,931 --> 00:00:57,764
The angle goes back and forth,
and the dot follows the bob.

25
00:00:58,164 --> 00:00:59,208
Slow-mo replay.

26
00:00:59,208 --> 00:01:01,813
Gravity, in red, pulls the bob down.

27
00:01:01,813 --> 00:01:05,121
The velocity, in blue,
is biggest at the bottom.

28
00:01:05,521 --> 00:01:07,456
What could the period depend on?

29
00:01:07,456 --> 00:01:09,120
Only three things are here.

30
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:11,317
The mass of the bob, m.

31
00:01:11,317 --> 00:01:13,354
The length of the string, l.

32
00:01:13,354 --> 00:01:14,590
And gravity, g.

33
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:17,908
Let's build the formula,
one step at a time.

34
00:01:18,258 --> 00:01:19,158
Step one.

35
00:01:19,099 --> 00:01:23,732
Guess T equals k, times m to
the x, l to the y, g to the z.

36
00:01:23,732 --> 00:01:25,269
k is a pure number.

37
00:01:26,019 --> 00:01:26,919
Step two.

38
00:01:26,878 --> 00:01:27,779
Dimensions.

39
00:01:27,779 --> 00:01:29,026
T on the left.

40
00:01:29,026 --> 00:01:33,824
M to the x, L to the y, and
LT to the minus two, to the z.

41
00:01:34,574 --> 00:01:35,535
Step three.

42
00:01:35,535 --> 00:01:37,256
Match the powers of M.

43
00:01:37,256 --> 00:01:40,397
There is no M on the left, so x is zero.

44
00:01:41,147 --> 00:01:42,194
Step four.

45
00:01:42,194 --> 00:01:44,178
Match the powers of T.

46
00:01:44,178 --> 00:01:46,223
One equals minus two z.

47
00:01:46,223 --> 00:01:48,340
So z is minus one half.

48
00:01:49,090 --> 00:01:50,092
Step five.

49
00:01:50,092 --> 00:01:51,994
Match the powers of L.

50
00:01:51,994 --> 00:01:53,837
Zero equals y plus z.

51
00:01:53,837 --> 00:01:55,808
So y is plus one half.

52
00:01:56,558 --> 00:02:00,082
So T equals k times the
square root of l over g.

53
00:02:00,082 --> 00:02:02,874
We found it without a single experiment.

54
00:02:03,574 --> 00:02:06,182
Now read what this formula is telling us.

55
00:02:06,182 --> 00:02:07,383
Follow the links.

56
00:02:07,733 --> 00:02:10,117
x is zero, so mass has no power.

57
00:02:10,117 --> 00:02:12,804
So the mass of the bob does not matter.

58
00:02:13,204 --> 00:02:17,141
y is one half, so T grows with
the square root of the length.

59
00:02:17,541 --> 00:02:19,243
z is minus one half.

60
00:02:19,243 --> 00:02:22,230
So stronger gravity means a faster swing.

61
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:23,961
Side by side.

62
00:02:23,961 --> 00:02:25,551
Two one metre strings.

63
00:02:25,551 --> 00:02:28,493
A light key ring, and a heavy steel ball.

64
00:02:28,893 --> 00:02:31,746
Both take about two
seconds for a full swing.

65
00:02:31,746 --> 00:02:34,064
The heavy one does not swing faster.

66
00:02:34,664 --> 00:02:35,678
Now you try.

67
00:02:35,678 --> 00:02:38,354
Aarav makes the string four times longer.

68
00:02:38,354 --> 00:02:40,223
What happens to the period?

69
00:02:40,223 --> 00:02:42,093
Pause the video and decide.

70
00:02:45,293 --> 00:02:46,193
It doubles!

71
00:02:46,178 --> 00:02:50,663
T goes as the square root of l,
and the square root of four is two.

72
00:02:50,663 --> 00:02:52,338
Two seconds becomes four.

73
00:02:52,988 --> 00:02:54,593
Now the second use.

74
00:02:54,593 --> 00:02:55,953
Converting units.

75
00:02:56,303 --> 00:02:59,963
In the SI system, energy
is measured in joules.

76
00:02:59,963 --> 00:03:02,403
The older CGS system uses ergs.

77
00:03:02,803 --> 00:03:06,522
Energy has dimensions ML
squared T to the minus two.

78
00:03:06,922 --> 00:03:11,761
So the number changes by the mass
ratio, times the length ratio squared,

79
00:03:11,761 --> 00:03:14,510
times the time ratio to the minus two.

80
00:03:15,260 --> 00:03:16,189
Your turn.

81
00:03:16,189 --> 00:03:20,760
Which ratio goes in: old unit
over new unit, or new over old?

82
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:22,127
Pause and decide.

83
00:03:25,327 --> 00:03:26,487
Old over new!

84
00:03:26,487 --> 00:03:29,719
n two equals n one times u one over u two.

85
00:03:29,719 --> 00:03:32,930
A bigger old unit gives
a bigger new number.

86
00:03:33,580 --> 00:03:35,316
Now, one worked example.

87
00:03:35,666 --> 00:03:37,564
How many ergs make one joule?

88
00:03:38,064 --> 00:03:40,630
One kilogram is one thousand grams.

89
00:03:40,630 --> 00:03:43,306
One metre is one hundred centimetres.

90
00:03:43,306 --> 00:03:45,318
The second stays the same.

91
00:03:46,068 --> 00:03:50,661
n two equals one, times one thousand,
times one hundred squared, times

92
00:03:50,661 --> 00:03:51,561
one.

93
00:03:51,923 --> 00:03:54,511
That is one thousand times ten thousand.

94
00:03:54,511 --> 00:03:56,159
Ten to the power seven.

95
00:03:56,159 --> 00:03:58,767
So one joule is ten to the seven ergs.

96
00:03:59,417 --> 00:04:00,882
Now, the exam trap.

97
00:04:01,232 --> 00:04:06,040
Many students flip the ratio, and get
one newton equals ten to the minus

98
00:04:06,040 --> 00:04:07,004
five dynes.

99
00:04:07,404 --> 00:04:09,817
But a dyne is a much smaller force.

100
00:04:09,817 --> 00:04:11,755
Smaller unit, bigger number.

101
00:04:11,755 --> 00:04:14,568
One newton is ten to the power five dynes.

102
00:04:15,218 --> 00:04:16,687
Where is this asked?

103
00:04:16,687 --> 00:04:21,461
As questions that build a formula from
what a quantity depends on, or convert

104
00:04:21,461 --> 00:04:22,361
units.

105
00:04:22,663 --> 00:04:23,954
Exam level now.

106
00:04:23,954 --> 00:04:28,783
Ripple speed depends on surface
tension S, density rho, and wavelength

107
00:04:28,783 --> 00:04:29,683
lambda.

108
00:04:29,505 --> 00:04:31,016
Which formula fits?

109
00:04:31,016 --> 00:04:32,363
Pause and solve.

110
00:04:37,763 --> 00:04:39,082
The answer is B.

111
00:04:39,082 --> 00:04:41,722
The square root of S over rho lambda.

112
00:04:42,222 --> 00:04:46,111
Surface tension is force per
length, MT to the minus two.

113
00:04:46,111 --> 00:04:49,099
Matching T gives the
power of S as one half.

114
00:04:49,599 --> 00:04:52,784
Matching M gives rho the
power minus one half.

115
00:04:52,784 --> 00:04:55,752
Matching L gives lambda
minus one half too.

116
00:04:56,352 --> 00:04:57,811
Here is your recap.

117
00:04:57,811 --> 00:04:59,311
Screenshot this card.

118
00:05:02,511 --> 00:05:05,348
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

119
00:05:05,748 --> 00:05:06,648
One.

120
00:05:06,228 --> 00:05:10,614
Why does the mass of the bob not
appear in the formula for the period?

121
00:05:11,514 --> 00:05:12,414
Two.

122
00:05:11,999 --> 00:05:16,305
If the string is made nine times
longer, what happens to the period?

123
00:05:17,205 --> 00:05:18,105
Three.

124
00:05:17,799 --> 00:05:20,456
How many ergs make one joule, and why?

125
00:05:21,556 --> 00:05:25,102
Next time: our formula
had a mystery number k.

126
00:05:25,102 --> 00:05:26,926
Can dimensions find it?

127
00:05:26,926 --> 00:05:29,472
Limitations of dimensional analysis.
